// Pastura/Pastura/Resources/Presets/ (prisoners_dilemma_en / word_wolf_en /
// bokete_en / target_score_race_en); the how-to and observation tips mirror
// docs/examples/scenarios/README.md. Per .claude/rules/lp-content.md, this new
// copy avoids em-dash / prose-colon and does NOT use the AIgazing genre word.
---

<BaseLayout
  locale="en"
  title="Pastura Scenario Guide"
  description="An introduction to Pastura scenarios. Learn what they are, how to write one, and see example experiments you can run on-device."
  enPath="/docs/"
  jaPath="/ja/docs/"
  variant="prose"
>
  <a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a>

  <div class="page-shell">
    <header role="banner" class="lp-header">
      <a href="../" class="brand">
        <img class="icon" src="../img/app-icon.png" alt="" aria-hidden="true" width="36" height="36">
        <span class="brand-name">Pastura</span>
      </a>
      <nav aria-label="Primary" class="lp-nav">
        <div class="lang-toggle" role="group" aria-label="Language">
          <a lang="en" hreflang="en" aria-current="page" class="on" href="./">EN</a>
          <a lang="ja" hreflang="ja" href="../ja/docs/">JA</a>
        </div>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <main id="main" class="page-main">
      <div class="page-prose">
        <h1>Scenario guide</h1>

        <p class="lead">
          Pastura runs small multi-agent experiments on your device. You write a
          scenario in YAML, and a local language model acts out every agent in it.
          This page walks through what a scenario looks like and how to write one.
        </p>

        <h2 id="how-it-works">How a scenario works</h2>

        <p>
          A scenario is a single YAML file. It declares how many agents take part,
          who they are, and what they do each round. Three pieces carry most of the
          meaning:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li><code>agents</code> and <code>rounds</code> set the size of the run.</li>
          <li><code>personas</code> give each agent a name and a short character brief.</li>
          <li>
            <code>phases</code> describe what happens in order, such as everyone
            speaking, making a choice, voting, or tallying a score.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          The app ships with several built-in scenarios, so you can watch a run
          before writing anything of your own.
        </p>

        <h2 id="example">A worked example</h2>

        <p>
          The built-in <strong>Prisoner&rsquo;s Dilemma</strong> puts five
          contestants through a classic strategy game. Each round they address the
          group, then choose to cooperate or betray each opponent in turn. A scoring
          phase converts those choices into points, and bluffing during the spoken
          phase is fair play. Here is the shape of it in YAML:
        </p>

        <pre><code>name: Prisoner's Dilemma
agents: 5
rounds: 3
context: &gt;
  You are a contestant on the game show "Prisoner's Dilemma".
  Against each opponent you choose "cooperate" or "betray".
  Both cooperate = 3 points each. You alone betray = 5 points.

personas:
  - name: Alex
    description: &gt;
      [Role] Calm strategist.
      [Goal] Compute the optimal move from data and past behaviour.
  - name: Mia
    description: &gt;
      [Role] Optimistic people-pleaser.
      [Goal] Trust people by default, even after being burned.

phases:
  - type: speak_all      # everyone addresses the group
  - type: choose         # pick cooperate or betray, per opponent
    options: [cooperate, betray]
    pairing: round_robin
  - type: score_calc     # built-in prisoners_dilemma scoring
    logic: prisoners_dilemma
  - type: summarize</code></pre>

        <p>
          Five personas, three rounds, four phases. The persona briefs are where the
          run gets its character. A calm strategist and an optimistic people-pleaser
          will read the same scoreboard and reach for very different moves.
        </p>

        <h2 id="more-examples">More examples</h2>

        <p>
          The other built-in scenarios each highlight a different mechanic. Every one
          uses only built-in scoring, so they run out of the box.
        </p>

        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Scenario</th>
              <th>What you can watch for</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Word Wolf</strong></td>
              <td>
                Five players get a topic word, but one of them gets a different word.
                Watch how the odd one out hides, and whether the group exposes them in
                the vote.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Bokete</strong></td>
              <td>
                Five comedians caption a photo prompt, then vote on the funniest bit.
                Watch how each persona&rsquo;s comedic style holds up under a crowd vote.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Score Race</strong></td>
              <td>
                A vote-driven points race to a target score. The round-end summary
                changes once someone reaches the target, so you can see how a phase
                branches on game state.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h2 id="writing">Writing your own</h2>

        <p>
          Every built-in scenario is a starting point. Open one in the scenario
          editor, change a persona or a phase, and run it again to see how the
          outcome shifts. A few things are worth doing as you experiment:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>
            Run the same scenario several times. The model is non-deterministic, so a
            single run never tells the whole story. Look for patterns across runs.
          </li>
          <li>
            Vary a persona&rsquo;s description. Adding a trait like &ldquo;confident&rdquo;
            or &ldquo;eager to please&rdquo; can change how the run plays out.
          </li>
          <li>
            Reveal the inner thoughts. What an agent was privately thinking is often
            more telling than what it said out loud.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="get-the-scenarios">Get the scenarios</h2>

        <p>
          Pastura is coming soon to the App Store. In the meantime, the full YAML for
          every built-in scenario lives in the project repository, ready to read or
          adapt.
        </p>

        <p>
          <a href="https://github.com/tyabu12/pastura/tree/main/Pastura/Pastura/Resources/Presets" rel="noopener">Browse the bundled presets on GitHub</a>,
          or head <a href="../">back to the Pastura overview</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
